    <h1>State-Regulated Life Insurance: The Government Benefit Hoax</h1>
    <p class="inv__byline">By Randy VanderVaate, licensed life insurance agent, Founder of Funeral Funds</p>

    <div class="inv__opening">
      <p><strong>State-regulated life insurance. Sounds like some special government program, doesn't it?</strong></p>
      <p>Well, I've got news for you.</p>
      <p>There is no special government benefit hiding behind those words.</p>
      <p>Every legitimate life insurance company selling policies in your state is regulated by the state. That's how insurance works.</p>
      <p>So why are these companies mailing out official-looking cards talking about state-regulated programs and benefits up to $25,000?</p>
      <p><strong>Because they want your information.</strong></p>
      <p>Your name. Your address. Your phone number. Sometimes even your spouse's information.</p>
      <p>And once you've handed it over, that information can become a sales lead.</p>
      <p>That's the trick.</p>
      <p>They're not sending you a check for $25,000. They're trying to get you to respond to an insurance advertisement.</p>
      <p>And I think dressing up an ordinary insurance sales pitch to look like some special government benefit is a pretty lousy way to do business.</p>
      <p>Now take a look at this actual mailer. I've numbered eight things on it that I think every senior ought to see.</p>
    </div>

    <MailerDecoder part="figure" />

    <MailerDecoder part="claims" />

    <h2>What happens when you send the card back</h2>
    <p>Some companies specialize in mailing these cards and selling the replies to insurance agents as leads. In my experience, an agent may pay around $40, $50 or $60 for each lead, or some other amount, depending on the lead program. The agent who buys your lead may represent one insurance company or several. What you're offered depends on which products that agent has, how well the agent knows underwriting (which companies accept which health conditions), and your own health and budget.</p>
    <p>Some agents may lean toward guaranteed-acceptance insurance because it doesn't ask health questions and can be easier to place. That isn't true of every agent, and you can't tell how an agent works, or how they're paid, just from the mailer. Guaranteed acceptance can be the right fit for someone who can't qualify for simplified issue coverage. But it generally costs more than first-day coverage for a comparable applicant, and it has a waiting period before the full benefit is paid for a death from natural causes.</p>

    <h2>Is your life insurance premium taking food off your table?</h2>
    <p>A premium difference is easy to ignore at $38 a month. So here's the same money as groceries. Over 10 years, $4,602 would buy roughly 153 days of food for one person: about 307,000 calories, or 460 meals at $10 and 667 calories a meal, at 2,000 calories a day. That's illustrative purchasing power, what the money could buy. It isn't a claim about anyone's diet, and it only counts as a saving if you qualify for the lower-priced plan.</p>
    <p>The calculator starts with the lowest price shown as the baseline. Pick any other plan in the chart to compare it, choose a period, or change the age and coverage:</p>

    <ImpactCalculator variant="embedded" title="Financial Impact Calculator" />

    <p class="inv__video" role="note"><strong>Video {video(1).n} of 5: {video(1).title}</strong> <span>The card, line by line, in about a minute.</span></p>

    <h2 id="regulators">What regulators found about mailers like this</h2>
    <p>I can't tell you who sent the card in this report, and I'm not going to guess. What I can show you is what state insurance regulators documented about other mailers that use the same style.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Washington, D.C. (2020), consumer warning.</strong> The D.C. insurance department (DISB) warned residents about a mailer titled "F-1 2020 Benefit Information for District of Columbia Residents Only." DISB said the mailer was not from the District government and was not sanctioned by DISB, and that the information people send back may be used by, or sold to, insurance companies so their agents can contact them.</li>
      <li><strong>Iowa (2020), enforcement action.</strong> Two Georgia companies, Direct Mail Processing, LLC and Rehg Data Assets, Inc., sent official-looking insurance solicitation mailers to older Iowans. One mailer referred to a "state-regulated program" for final expenses. People who returned a card got a sales call from an insurance salesperson, and the mailer company was paid for each lead. The Iowa Insurance Division issued a cease-and-desist order against the operation. Separately, the Iowa Attorney General's Office reached a consent judgment with the two companies, which agreed to pay the state $40,000 and denied violating Iowa's Consumer Fraud Act.</li>
      <li><strong>Nebraska (2022), consumer education.</strong> In a presentation on misleading insurance marketing, the Nebraska Department of Insurance showed a T-2 mailer headed "2020 Benefit Information for Nebraska Citizens Only" and explained how lead generation works.</li>
      <li>The Texas Department of Insurance puts it plainly in its consumer fraud guide: "The government doesn't sell insurance. Agents who say they're with the government are breaking the law."</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These findings are about those mailers. They don't identify the sender of the 2026 card above, and they aren't evidence that its sender broke any law.</p>

    <p class="inv__video" role="note"><strong>Video {video(2).n} of 5: {video(2).title}</strong> <span>What D.C., Iowa and Nebraska found.</span></p>

    <h2 id="big-check">The "big check" versus what the policy actually pays</h2>
    <p>"Up to $25,000, tax-free" reads like money on its way. It isn't. Twenty-five thousand dollars is the most coverage the policy would offer, and you'd pay a monthly premium for it. "Tax-free" isn't a perk for state residents either: life insurance paid to a beneficiary because of a death generally isn't taxable income, whatever state you live in (IRS Publication 525).</p>
    <p>Your state doesn't sponsor, fund or give away private life insurance to its residents. The real government options are limited and specific: Social Security's one-time $255 death payment to an eligible spouse or child, VA burial benefits and VALife for eligible veterans, and a few others for particular groups.</p>
    <p>What matters is what the policy you're offered actually pays, and when. These are the main types:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Level (first-day coverage):</strong> the full death benefit from the first day, as long as the premiums are paid, subject to the policy's exclusions. You answer health questions to qualify.</li>
      <li><strong>Graded:</strong> a reduced benefit for a death from natural causes in the early years, then the full benefit.</li>
      <li><strong>Modified:</strong> during the waiting period, a refund of premiums plus an additional amount set by the policy instead of the full benefit.</li>
      <li><strong>Guaranteed acceptance:</strong> no health questions, and a waiting period. If you die from natural causes during the waiting period, your beneficiary receives a refund of the premiums paid plus an additional amount specified in the policy. Depending on the insurance company, that additional amount is commonly around 7% to 10%, and the exact refund calculation is set by each company's contract.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Here's what that waiting period means in dollars. A 65-year-old woman with Gerber Life Guaranteed Life at $10,000 pays $62.61 a month: about $1,503 over two years. If she died of natural causes in month 18, her family would receive her premiums paid plus 10%, about $1,240, instead of $10,000. On a first-day plan she qualified for, they'd receive the full $10,000.</p>

    <p class="inv__video" role="note"><strong>Video {video(3).n} of 5: {video(3).title}</strong> <span>"Up to $25,000, tax-free," explained.</span></p>

    <h2 id="real-cost">The real cost: guaranteed acceptance compared with first-day coverage</h2>
    <p>I'm not saying any mailer leads to these particular companies. I'm using them because their rates are published and verified, so you can see what the choices cost for the same person.</p>
    <p>For a 65-year-old woman who doesn't use tobacco, buying about $10,000 of coverage (Texas rates):</p>
    <div class="inv__scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Monthly premiums, 65-year-old woman">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Plan</th><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Coverage</th><th scope="col">Monthly premium</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Colonial Penn Guaranteed Acceptance</th><td>Guaranteed acceptance, 2-year limited benefit period</td><td>8 units = $10,064</td><td>$79.60</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Gerber Life Guaranteed Life</th><td>Guaranteed acceptance, 2-year waiting period</td><td>$10,000</td><td>$62.61</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Aetna Accendo Final Expense Preferred</th><td>Level, first-day coverage</td><td>$10,000</td><td>$47.60</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Mutual of Omaha Living Promise Level</th><td>Level, first-day coverage</td><td>$10,000</td><td>$41.01</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>At Colonial Penn's exact coverage of $10,064, Mutual of Omaha Living Promise Level is $41.25 a month. That's a difference of $38.35 a month, $460.20 a year, $4,602 over 10 years and $9,204 over 20 years, for someone who qualifies for the first-day plan.</p>
    <p>The gap grows with age. At 80, Gerber Life Guaranteed Life at $10,000 costs a woman $176.37 a month and a man $247.32, against $98.43 and $139.73 for Mutual of Omaha Living Promise Level. And what one $9.95 Colonial Penn unit buys shrinks from $1,761 of coverage for a 55-year-old woman to $608 at 80.</p>

    <p class="inv__video" role="note"><strong>Video {video(4).n} of 5: {video(4).title}</strong> <span>The calculator: premiums and days of food.</span></p>

    <h2 id="better-decision">Before you respond to that mailer, do this instead</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>You don't have to answer the card.</li>
      <li>If an agent calls, ask which insurance company the policy is with, what type of coverage it is, and whether it has a waiting period. Ask what your family would receive if you died in the first two years.</li>
      <li>Check that the agent and the company are licensed through your state's insurance department.</li>
      <li>Answer the health questions before you settle for a waiting period. Your actual price and eligibility depend on your age, health, coverage amount and the insurance company.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The vast majority of the people I help qualify for first-day coverage. My process is simple. I ask a few short health questions, compare rates from around 20 to 30 insurance companies, depending on your state, age, and health, and show you the first-day coverage options available to you. If you'd like me to look at an offer you received, call me at <a href={lead.phone.href}>{lead.phone.display}</a>.</p>

    <p class="inv__video" role="note"><strong>Video {video(5).n} of 5: {video(5).title}</strong> <span>Four questions to ask before you buy.</span></p>

